WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.126
<v Examiner>Four marks.

2
00:00:02.126 --> 00:00:07.137
<v Examiner>Define informed consent, and discuss the nurse's role in the consent process,

3
00:00:07.137 --> 00:00:09.154
<v Examiner>including who can give consent.

4
00:00:09.604 --> 00:00:13.634
Informed consent is the process of telling a patient the benefits,

5
00:00:13.634 --> 00:00:16.748
risks and alternatives of a procedure or treatment,

6
00:00:16.748 --> 00:00:20.289
so they can make an informed decision to accept it or not.

7
00:00:20.289 --> 00:00:24.929
It's needed for every healthcare procedure, and it can be written or verbal.

8
00:00:24.929 --> 00:00:30.241
The law behind it: Right seven of the Code of Health and Disability Services Consumers'

9
00:00:30.241 --> 00:00:34.576
Rights, the right to make an informed choice and give informed consent;

10
00:00:34.576 --> 00:00:39.949
and section eleven of the New Zealand Bill of Rights Act, the right to refuse treatment.

11
00:00:40.399 --> 00:00:41.477
Who can consent.

12
00:00:41.477 --> 00:00:46.194
Anyone sixteen or over can consent to, or refuse, their own treatment.

13
00:00:46.194 --> 00:00:48.890
If someone over sixteen isn't competent,

14
00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:54.147
a legal guardian or someone with a current enduring power of attorney decides.

15
00:00:54.147 --> 00:00:56.910
For under-sixteens, a parent or guardian.

16
00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:58.056
Or a court order.

17
00:00:58.056 --> 00:01:02.571
A patient committed under the Mental Health Act 1992 can't consent,

18
00:01:02.571 --> 00:01:08.501
but they still have the right to be told about their treatment in a way they understand,

19
00:01:08.501 --> 00:01:10.658
and the right to an interpreter.

20
00:01:11.108 --> 00:01:12.916
Now discuss the nurse's role.

21
00:01:12.916 --> 00:01:15.161
The nurse is the patient's advocate.

22
00:01:15.161 --> 00:01:19.588
Arrange an interpreter if one's needed, a professional one, not family.

23
00:01:19.588 --> 00:01:22.643
Make sure whānau support is there if appropriate.

24
00:01:22.643 --> 00:01:28.005
Check the medical team has given all the information in a way the patient understands,

25
00:01:28.005 --> 00:01:29.689
and if not, call them back.

26
00:01:29.689 --> 00:01:34.864
Make sure the patient has had a chance to ask questions, and check they understand.

27
00:01:34.864 --> 00:01:37.420
Know enough about the procedure yourself.

28
00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:40.413
And sometimes the nurse witnesses the signature,

29
00:01:40.413 --> 00:01:45.463
confirming the person signed in their presence and is of legal age and competent.

30
00:01:45.913 --> 00:01:47.794
Two scenarios the quiz uses.

31
00:01:47.794 --> 00:01:52.161
A patient changes their mind halfway through a blood transfusion:

32
00:01:52.161 --> 00:01:55.990
consent can be withdrawn at any time, so stop and notify.

33
00:01:55.990 --> 00:01:59.887
And consent is obtained by the person doing the procedure.

34
00:02:00.337 --> 00:02:05.183
Informed consent: benefits, risks and alternatives, so the patient can decide;

35
00:02:05.183 --> 00:02:07.358
every procedure, written or verbal.

36
00:02:07.358 --> 00:02:12.080
Right seven of the Code; Bill of Rights section eleven, the right to refuse.

37
00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:14.566
Sixteen and over consent for themselves.

38
00:02:14.566 --> 00:02:19.288
Nurse is the advocate: professional interpreter, whānau, call the team back,

39
00:02:19.288 --> 00:02:20.530
check understanding.

40
00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:23.078
Withdrawn mid-procedure: stop and notify.
